hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1211","###\n Bunnad narrated to us, Muhammad narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, who said: I heard 'Asim ibn Damrah say: I asked 'Ali (RA) about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he mentioned this hadith [219].\n\nAbu Bakr (the commentator) said: \"In this report from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA), it is mentioned that he prayed two rak'ahs during the day"
"1212","Shu'bah bin Al-Hajjaj narrated, on the authority of Abd Rabbuh bin Saeed, on the authority of Anas bin Abi Anas, on the authority of Abdullah bin Nafi bin Al-Amiya, On the authority of Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, on the authority of al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada`ah, that the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, said: “Prayer is two by two, And recite the Tashahud in every two rak'ahs, and smile, and remain still, and fold your hands, and say: 'O God, O God, whoever does not do so is a premature baby.'\" We told him Ali bin Khashram, Issa informed us, on the authority of Shu’bah, on the authority of Abd Rabbuh bin Sa’id"
"1213","Al-Layth bin Saad Shu’bah disagreed with the chain of transmission of this report, so Al-Layth narrated it, on the authority of Abd Rabbo, on the authority of Imran bin Abi Unais, on the authority of Abd Allah bin Nafi bin Al-Amiya, on the authority of Rabi’ah bin Al-Harith, on the authority of Al-Fadl bin Abbas, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. It was narrated to us by Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala, it was narrated to us by Yahya, meaning Ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr, it was narrated to us by Al-Layth. If this news is proven, then this is The word: “Prayer is two by two,” like the report of Ibn Omar, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. “And in this report there is an additional explanation of the mention of raising the hands to say: O God, O God,” and in the report of Al-Layth he said: “Raise them to your Lord, You face them with your face and say: O Lord, O Lord, and raising the hands in the tashahhud before the salutation is not part of the Sunnah of prayer, and this indicates that. He only commanded him to raise the hands, to supplicate, and to ask after the greeting from the two people. As for the report that some people used as evidence in The four times before noon, the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, prayed them with a salutation. It was narrated with a chain of transmission that no one like it can use as evidence. “Knowledge of telling news.”"
"1214","It was narrated to us by Ali bin Hajar, by Muhammad bin Yazid al-Wasiti, by Thanth Salam bin Junada ⦗222⦘, by Waki’, on the authority of Ubaidah bin Mu’tab al-Dhabi, On the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of Sahm bin Munjab, on the authority of Qaza’ah, on the authority of Al-Qarth’, on the authority of Abu Ayyub, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. Bundar told us, Abu Dawud told us, Shu’bah told us, Ubaidah told me - and it was from the beginning of his hadith - on the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of Sahm bin Munjab, On the authority of Qaza’ah, on the authority of Al-Qartha’, on the authority of Abu Ayyub: On the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, he said: “There are four days before noon in which the salutation is not opened. The gates of heaven. This is the pronunciation of the hadith of Shu`bah. As for Muhammad bin Yazid, he had a long hadith and mentioned many things in it. Then Bundar told us, on the authority of Muhammad, on the authority of Shu’bah, on the authority of Ubaidah ibn Utab, on the authority of Ibn Munjab, on the authority of a man, on the authority of Qarth al-Dhabi, on the authority of Abu Ayyub: On the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. Ubaidah bin Mu’tab, may God have mercy on him, is not one of those whose information may be used as evidence for those who have knowledge of the narrators of the news. And I heard Abu Musa say: I did not hear Yahya bin Saeed nor Abd al-Rahman bin Mahdi narrate on the authority of Sufyan, on the authority of Ubaidah bin Mu’tab. With nothing at all. And I heard Abu Qalaba narrating on the authority of Hilal bin Yahya, who said: I heard Yusuf bin Khaled Al-Samti saying: I said to Ubaidah bin Mut’ab: This is the one who Have you heard all of the narration on the authority of Abraham? He said: Some of it is what I heard, and some of it is what I can compare it to. He said: I said: So tell me what you heard, for I know better by analogy than you (*). He narrated something similar to this, Al-A’mash, on the authority of Al-Musayyab ibn Rafi’, on the authority of Ali ibn Al-Salt, on the authority of Abu Ayyub, on the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, Except that it does not include: He does not greet between them."
"1215","It was narrated to us by Ali ibn Hajar, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti, on the authority of Salam ibn Junada, on the authority of Waki’, on the authority of Ubaidah ibn Mu’tab al-Dhabi. On the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of Sahm bin Munjab, on the authority of Qaza’ah, on the authority of Al-Qarth’, on the authority of Abu Ayyub, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace. Bundar told us, Abu Dawud told us, Shu’bah told us, Ubaidah told me, and it was from the ancient times of his talk on the authority of Abraham, on the authority of Sahm bin Munjab, on the authority of Qaza’ah, on the authority of Al-Qurtha’, on the authority of Abu Ayyub, on the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, who said: “There are four days before noon in which one does not say the salaam.” The gates of heaven will be opened for them.” This is the pronunciation of the hadith of Shu`bah. As for Muhammad bin Yazid, he had a long hadith and mentioned many things in it. Then Bundar narrated to us, narrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us Shu`bah, on the authority of Ubaidah ibn Utab, on the authority of Ibn Munjab, on the authority of a man, on the authority of Qarth al-Dhabi, on the authority of Abu Ayyub, on the authority of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, something similar. Ubaidah bin Mu’tib, may God have mercy on him, is not one of those whose information may be used as evidence by someone who has knowledge of narrating news. And I heard Abu Musa saying: I did not hear Yahya bin Saeed nor Abd al-Rahman bin Mahdi narrate, on the authority of Sufyan, on the authority of Ubaidah bin Mu’tab. With nothing at all. And I heard Abu Qalaba narrating on the authority of Hilal bin Yahya, he said: I heard Yusuf bin Khaled Al-Samti, saying: I said to Ubaidah bin Mu’tab: This is the one who Have you heard all of the narration on the authority of Abraham? He said: Some of it is what I heard, and some of it is what I can compare to. He said: I said: So tell me what you heard, for I know better by analogy than you. He narrated something similar to this, Al-A’mash, on the authority of Al-Musayyab ibn Rafi’, on the authority of Ali ibn Al-Salt, on the authority of Abu Ayyub, on the authority of the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him. And he greeted, except that it does not include: he does not greet between them. Abu Musa narrated to us, Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Amash, H. Abu Musa narrated to us, Ma’mal bin Ismail narrated to us, Sufyan, on the authority of Al-A’mash, on the authority of Al-Musayyab bin Rafi’, on the authority of a man from the Ansar, on the authority of Abu Ayyub. Abu Bakr said: I do not know this Ali bin Al-Salt, and I do not know which country of God he is from, and I do not understand whether I should meet Abu Ayyub or not? Only a stubborn or ignorant person would cite such scientific chains of narration as evidence."
